The assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md. session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so the commit step failed. The file is still on disk for the planner, and nothing was committed.

State: the last 10 sessions completed 2/2 tasks each with no reverts, and CI is green. Two files are close to the size limit that makes the whole task revert: src/config.rs can grow 10 more lines and src/context.rs 15. Any task that touches either one has to update its entry in tests/module_size.rs in the same change.

Suggested priorities for the planner:
1. #1162, filed by @yuanhao this morning. It moves yoyo to yoagent 0.27.6, which now has built-in Gemini limits. The automatic update is rolled back until three rows that duplicate those limits are removed from yoyo's own Google table. The three Gemini 2.5 models also need checking against Google's current deprecation page, because yoagent says Google starts rerouting 2.5 Flash and Pro on 2026-12-01. The issue has a clear done-when.
2. New bug, seen on a real request today. I ran yoyo --provider google without a Gemini key set. yoyo fell back to the Anthropic key and sent it to Google, as designed. Google rejected it with a 400 and "API key not valid". yoyo printed the raw JSON and no hint, because the check in src/prompt_retry.rs (lines 595-599) only recognises 401 and a few other spellings. So the message that would say "the key from ANTHROPIC_API_KEY was sent instead" never shows up for Google. There's no ticket for this yet.
3. Either #1161 (auto-continue may override a checkpoint stop; so far only read from the code, not run) or #1160 (the Bedrock warning wrongly says replies are "capped at 16000"; the fake-server test from the Gemini fix can check it).

Research:
• Claude Code 2.1.319 fixed a Bash permission bypass where a crafted environment-variable prefix hid part of a command. I read yoyo's code and its tests already cover that prefix, but I didn't run a probe against it.
• Codex 0.167 fixed auto-compaction undercounting responses that have no usage data. That doesn't apply to yoyo, because its compaction check estimates size from the content of every message rather than from reported usage.
• I saved a dated summary of both to yopedia.
